PCB Defect Dataset Overview:
The dataset used in this project is adapted from the DeepPCB benchmark dataset, which is widely applied in automated optical inspection research. It provides a collection of paired images, where each pair contains a flawless PCB reference image and its corresponding defective PCB sample. Each defect sample is accompanied by annotation files that describe the location and type of defect present.
This dataset has been structured to support three major computer vision tasks:
Detecting whether a PCB contains a defect.
Pinpointing the exact location of defective regions using bounding boxes.
Training deep learning models to recognize different categories of PCB faults.
Defect Types and Sample Distribution

Six common types of PCB defects are included:
Missing Hole – Holes that should have been drilled are absent. (115 samples)
Mouse Bite – The PCB edge contains small notches or irregular cutouts. (115 samples)
Open Circuit – Copper traces are broken, causing gaps in connections. (116 samples)
Short Circuit – Extra copper causes unintended bridging between traces. (116 samples)
Spur – A thin stray copper trace sticks out from a connection. (115 samples)
Spurious Copper – Unwanted copper areas appear that are not part of the design. (116 samples)
In total, the dataset contains 693 defect images across all categories.

Data Augmentation:
To enhance model robustness, a separate rotation directory is included. This folder contains rotated versions of the defect samples. For each defect type, an *_angles.txt file (e.g., Spur_angles.txt) records the exact rotation angles applied to its samples.

Annotation Format:
Each defective image has a corresponding XML annotation file. These annotations provide:
The defect label (class name).
Bounding box coordinates marking the defective region.
Metadata related to the defect instance.

Applications:
This dataset is designed for building and evaluating machine learning pipelines in PCB quality inspection, particularly for defect detection, localization, and classification tasks. With a balanced distribution of six defect types and clear annotations, it serves as a reliable benchmark for deep learning research in manufacturing defect analysis.
